Title:
KKYUM.sys: kernel r/w for every local user, and the leak that died on 26200

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post analyzes a newly-seen WHQL-signed Windows kernel driver (KKYUM.sys) used by a cheat, which exposes unrestricted IOCTLs that allow any local user to read/write arbitrary memory in any process, including kernel memory, via `MmCopyVirtualMemory`. The author demonstrates straightforward token-stealing LPE when already elevated (admin + SeDebug enabled), then focuses on the harder problem: getting a reliable kernel address leak on Windows 11 build 26200 where common `NtQuerySystemInformation`-based leaks and other kernel pointer sources are scrubbed/zeroed. They show that the classic `sidt` IDT-base KASLR leak is effectively “dead” on 26200 (returns a decoy base that triggers bugchecks when dereferenced). To regain a kernel base without privileges, they chain a second LOLDriver (eneio64.sys, CVE-2020-12446) that maps large ranges of physical memory into user space, then scan RAM for a known `ntoskrnl` entry-point pointer to recover the kernel base and complete token theft using KKYUM. The write-up is useful for red teamers and exploit developers tracking modern Windows kernel pointer sanitization and practical BYOVD chaining, and for defenders validating blocklist gaps and detection opportunities.

Technical Focus:
- BYOVD with WHQL-signed vulnerable drivers (KKYUM.sys, eneio64.sys)
- Arbitrary kernel read/write via IOCTL + `MmCopyVirtualMemory`
- Windows 11 26200 kernel pointer sanitization / KASLR leak mitigations
- Token stealing via EPROCESS traversal (`PsInitialSystemProcess`, `ActiveProcessLinks`, `Token`)
- Physical memory mapping + scanning to recover `ntoskrnl` base (KASLR bypass)
- `sidt`/IDTR-based leak behavior changes and induced bugchecks

Use Cases:
- Local privilege escalation to SYSTEM using vulnerable signed drivers
- Building/validating BYOVD chains when single-driver exploitation lacks a kernel leak
- Testing Windows 11 26200 mitigations against common kernel address disclosure techniques
- Defensive validation: identify exploitable drivers not present in Microsoft’s vulnerable driver blocklist
- Creating detections around suspicious device opens/IOCTL patterns and physical-memory mapping behavior

Keywords:
KKYUM.sys, eneio64.sys, BYOVD, LOLDrivers, WHQL signed driver, Windows 11 26200, HVCI, VBS, IOCTL, MmCopyVirtualMemory, PsLookupProcessByProcessId, NtQuerySystemInformation, KASLR, kernel pointer sanitization, SIDT, IDT, EPROCESS, token stealing, PsInitialSystemProcess, ActiveProcessLinks, CVE-2020-12446, physical memory mapping, vulnerable driver blocklist, win32kfull, ValidateHwnd, DKOM